1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,550
Chapter 1: Overview of Obstetrics.

3
00:00:05,550 --> 00:00:17,512
Vital Statistics Pregnancy Rates In The United States Obstetrical Care Measures Timely Topics In Obstetrics References The science and clinical practice of obstetrics focuses on human reproduction.

4
00:00:17,512 --> 00:00:23,925
The specialty promotes the health and well-being of the pregnant woman and her fetus through quality perinatal care.

5
00:00:23,925 --> 00:00:33,937
Such care entails recognition and treatment of complications, supervision of labor and delivery, initial care of the newborn, and management of the puerperium.

6
00:00:33,937 --> 00:00:38,662
Postpartum care promotes health and provides family planning options.

7
00:00:38,662 --> 00:00:43,337
Evidence-based medicine dominates the modern practice of obstetrics.

8
00:00:43,337 --> 00:00:51,825
As described by Williams in this textbook’s first edition, we too strive to present the scientific evidence for current obstetrical care.

9
00:00:51,825 --> 00:00:56,462
Still, high-quality data do not support most recommendations .

10
00:00:56,462 --> 00:01:02,687
Thus, much of our practice stems from expert-based opinions and historical experiences .

11
00:01:02,687 --> 00:01:21,762
To help bridge knowledge gaps, we also rely on authoritative sources such as the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists and the Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine, as well as agencies such as the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) and National Institutes of Health (NIH).

12
00:01:21,762 --> 00:01:23,912
Vital Statistics.

13
00:01:23,912 --> 00:01:32,650
The importance of obstetrics is demonstrated by the use of maternal and neonatal outcomes as an index of health and life quality among nations.

14
00:01:32,650 --> 00:01:42,237
Intuitively, indices showing poor obstetrical and perinatal outcomes could be assumed to reflect medical care deficiencies for the entire population.

15
00:01:42,237 --> 00:01:50,075
The National Vital Statistics System of the United States collects statistics on births and deaths, including fetal deaths.

16
00:01:50,075 --> 00:02:05,900
Legal authority for collection resides individually with the 50 states; two regions—the District of Columbia and New York City; and five territories—American Samoa, Guam, the Northern Mariana Islands, Puerto Rico, and the Virgin Islands.

17
00:02:05,900 --> 00:02:15,037
The standard birth certificate includes information on medical and lifestyle risks, labor and delivery factors, and newborn characteristics.

18
00:02:15,037 --> 00:02:20,100
Importantly, the current death certificate contains a pregnancy checkbox .

19
00:02:20,100 --> 00:02:22,450
# Definitions.

20
00:02:22,450 --> 00:02:34,250
Standard definitions are encouraged by the World Health Organization as well as the American Academy of Pediatrics and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019a).

21
00:02:34,250 --> 00:02:40,012
Uniformity allows data comparison between states or regions and between countries.

22
00:02:40,012 --> 00:02:44,087
Still, not all definitions are uniformly applied.

23
00:02:44,087 --> 00:02:52,662
For example, uniformity is incomplete among states regarding birthweight and gestational age criteria for reporting fetal deaths .

24
00:02:52,662 --> 00:02:55,925
Not all states follow this recommendation.

25
00:02:55,925 --> 00:03:15,075
Specifically, 28 states stipulate that losses beginning at 20 weeks’ gestation should be recorded as fetal deaths; eight states report all products of conception as fetal deaths; and still others use a minimum birthweight of 350 g, 400 g, or 500 g to define fetal death.

26
00:03:15,075 --> 00:03:24,225
To further the confusion, the National Vital Statistics Reports tabulates fetal deaths from pregnancies that are 20 weeks’ gestation or older .

27
00:03:24,225 --> 00:03:36,137
This is problematic because the 50th percentile for fetal weight at 20 weeks approximates 325 to 350 g— considerably less than the 500-g definition.

28
00:03:36,137 --> 00:03:43,737
In fact, a birthweight of 500 g corresponds closely with the 50th percentile for 22 weeks’ gestation.

29
00:03:43,737 --> 00:03:51,350
Definitions recommended by the National Center for Health Statistics and the CDC are as follows: Perinatal period.

30
00:03:51,350 --> 00:03:58,762
The interval between the birth of a neonate born after 20 weeks’ gestation and the 28 completed days after that birth.

31
00:03:58,762 --> 00:04:09,325
When perinatal rates are based on birthweight, rather than gestational age, recommendations define the perinatal period as commencing at the birth of a 500-g neonate.

32
00:04:09,325 --> 00:04:10,712
Birth.

33
00:04:10,712 --> 00:04:16,662
The complete expulsion or extraction from the mother of a fetus after 20 weeks’ gestation.

34
00:04:16,662 --> 00:04:29,512
As described above, in the absence of accurate dating criteria, fetuses weighing <500 g are usually not considered births but rather are termed abortuses for purposes of vital statistics.

35
00:04:29,512 --> 00:04:31,112
Birthweight.

36
00:04:31,112 --> 00:04:34,487
Neonatal weight determined immediately after

37
00:04:34,636 --> 00:04:37,948
delivery or as soon thereafter as feasible.

38
00:04:37,898 --> 00:04:40,948
It should be expressed to the nearest gram.

39
00:04:40,948 --> 00:04:42,598
Birth rate.

40
00:04:42,598 --> 00:04:46,548
The number of live births per 1000 population.

41
00:04:46,548 --> 00:04:48,498
Fertility rate.

42
00:04:48,498 --> 00:04:54,336
The number of live births per 1000 females aged 15 through 44 years.

43
00:04:54,336 --> 00:04:56,086
Live birth.

44
00:04:56,086 --> 00:05:08,186
The term used to record a birth whenever the newborn at or sometime after birth breathes spontaneously or shows any other sign of life such as a heartbeat or definite spontaneous movement of voluntary muscles.

45
00:05:08,186 --> 00:05:17,486
Heartbeats are distinguished from transient cardiac contractions, and respirations are differentiated from fleeting respiratory efforts or gasps.

46
00:05:17,486 --> 00:05:19,886
Stillbirth or fetal death.

47
00:05:19,886 --> 00:05:22,861
The absence of signs of life at birth.

48
00:05:22,861 --> 00:05:25,136
Early neonatal death.

49
00:05:25,136 --> 00:05:29,536
Death of a liveborn neonate during the first 7 days after birth.

50
00:05:29,536 --> 00:05:31,786
Late neonatal death.

51
00:05:31,786 --> 00:05:35,636
Death after 7 days but before 29 days.

52
00:05:35,636 --> 00:05:38,586
Stillbirth rate or fetal death rate.

53
00:05:38,586 --> 00:05:45,436
The number of stillborn neonates per 1000 neonates born, including live births and stillbirths.

54
00:05:45,436 --> 00:05:47,961
Neonatal mortality rate.

55
00:05:47,961 --> 00:05:52,111
The number of neonatal deaths per 1000 live births.

56
00:05:52,111 --> 00:05:54,623
Perinatal mortality rate.

57
00:05:54,623 --> 00:05:59,798
The number of stillbirths plus neonatal deaths per 1000 total births.

58
00:05:59,798 --> 00:06:01,586
Infant death.

59
00:06:01,586 --> 00:06:05,986
All deaths of liveborn infants from birth through 12 months of age.

60
00:06:05,986 --> 00:06:08,286
Infant mortality rate.

61
00:06:08,286 --> 00:06:12,211
The number of infant deaths per 1000 live births.

62
00:06:12,211 --> 00:06:14,098
Low birthweight.

63
00:06:14,098 --> 00:06:19,611
A newborn whose weight is <2500 g. Very low birthweight.

64
00:06:19,611 --> 00:06:25,373
A newborn whose weight is <1500 g. Extremely low birthweight.

65
00:06:25,373 --> 00:06:29,873
A newborn whose weight is <1000 g. Term neonate.

66
00:06:29,873 --> 00:06:40,498
A neonate born any time after 37 completed weeks’ gestation and up until 42 completed weeks’ gestation (260 to 294 days).

67
00:06:40,498 --> 00:06:51,311
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists and Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine encourage specific gestational age designations (2019a).

68
00:06:51,311 --> 00:06:58,348
Early term refers to neonates born at 37 completed weeks up to 386/7 weeks.

69
00:06:58,348 --> 00:07:04,661
Full term denotes those born at 39 completed weeks up to 406/7 weeks.

70
00:07:04,661 --> 00:07:11,673
Last, late term describes neonates born at 41 completed weeks up to 416/7 weeks.

71
00:07:11,673 --> 00:07:13,686
Preterm neonate.

72
00:07:13,686 --> 00:07:19,198
A neonate born before 37 completed weeks (the 259th day).

73
00:07:19,198 --> 00:07:28,973
A neonate born before 34 completed weeks is early preterm, whereas a neonate born between 34 and 36 completed weeks is late preterm.

74
00:07:28,973 --> 00:07:31,073
Postterm neonate.

75
00:07:31,073 --> 00:07:37,986
A neonate born any time after completion of the 42nd week, beginning with day 295.

76
00:07:37,986 --> 00:07:39,623
Abortus.

77
00:07:39,623 --> 00:07:54,148
A fetus or embryo removed or expelled from the uterus in the first half of gestation—20 weeks or less, or in the absence of accurate dating criteria, born weighing <500 g. Induced termination of pregnancy.

78
00:07:54,148 --> 00:08:03,136
The purposeful interruption of an intrauterine pregnancy that has the intention other than to produce a liveborn neonate and that does not result in a live birth.

79
00:08:03,136 --> 00:08:08,698
This definition excludes retention of products of conception following fetal death.

80
00:08:08,698 --> 00:08:10,961
Direct maternal death.

81
00:08:10,961 --> 00:08:24,036
The death of the mother that results from obstetrical complications of pregnancy, labor, or the puerperium and from interventions, omissions, incorrect treatment, or a chain of events resulting from any of these factors.

82
00:08:24,036 --> 00:08:28,873
An example is maternal death from exsanguination after uterine rupture.

83
00:08:28,873 --> 00:08:31,236
Indirect maternal death.

84
00:08:31,236 --> 00:08:35,811
A maternal death that is not directly due to an obstetrical cause.

85
00:08:35,811 --> 00:08:47,536
Death results from previously existing disease or a disease developing during pregnancy, labor, or the puerperium that was aggravated by maternal physiological adaptation to pregnancy.

86
00:08:47,536 --> 00:08:52,686
An example is maternal death from complications of mitral valve stenosis.

87
00:08:52,686 --> 00:08:54,786
Late maternal death.

88
00:08:54,786 --> 00:09:03,011
Death of a woman from direct or indirect obstetrical causes more than 42 days but less than 1 year after the pregnancy’s end.

89
00:09:03,011 --> 00:09:05,098
Nonmaternal death.

90
00:09:05,098 --> 00:09:11,173
Death of the mother that results from accidental or incidental causes not related to pregnancy.

91
00:09:11,173 --> 00:09:16,311
An example is death from an automobile accident or concurrent malignancy.

92
00:09:16,311 --> 00:09:18,923
Pregnancy-associated death.

93
00:09:18,923 --> 00:09:28,761
The death of a woman, from any cause, while pregnant or within 1 calendar year of termination of pregnancy, regardless of the duration and the site of pregnancy.

94
00:09:28,761 --> 00:09:31,136
Pregnancy-related death.

95
00:09:31,136 --> 00:09:40,236
A pregnancy-associated death that results from: (1) complications of pregnancy itself, (2) the chain of events initiated by

96
00:09:40,396 --> 00:09:51,483
pregnancy that led to death, or (3) aggravation of an unrelated condition by the physiological or pharmacological effects of pregnancy and that subsequently caused death.

97
00:09:51,433 --> 00:09:54,033
Maternal mortality ratio.

98
00:09:54,033 --> 00:10:00,433
The number of maternal deaths that result from the reproductive process per 100,000 live births.

99
00:10:00,433 --> 00:10:07,321
Used more commonly, but less accurately, are the terms maternal mortality rate or maternal death rate.

100
00:10:07,321 --> 00:10:22,546
The term ratio is more accurate because it includes in the numerator the number of deaths regardless of pregnancy outcome—for example, live births, stillbirths, and ectopic pregnancies—whereas the denominator includes the number of live births.

101
00:10:22,546 --> 00:10:25,558
Pregnancy Rates In The United States.

102
00:10:25,558 --> 00:10:36,196
According to the CDC, the fertility rate of women aged 15 to 44 years in the United States in 2019 was 58 live births per 1000 women.

103
00:10:36,196 --> 00:10:44,858
This rate began slowly trending downward in 1990 and has now dropped below that for replacement births to sustain the population level.

104
00:10:44,858 --> 00:10:47,958
This indicates a population decline.

105
00:10:47,958 --> 00:10:57,396
The birth rate decreased for all major ethnic and racial groups, for adolescents and unmarried women, and for those aged 20 to 24 years.

106
00:10:57,396 --> 00:11:01,721
For women older than 30 years, the birth rate rose slightly.

107
00:11:01,721 --> 00:11:13,258
Almost half of newborns in 2019 in the United States were minorities: Hispanic—25 percent; African-American—15 percent; and Asian—4 percent .

108
00:11:13,258 --> 00:11:19,771
The total number of pregnancies and their outcomes in 2019 are shown in Table 1-1.

109
00:11:19,771 --> 00:11:28,796
According to the Guttmacher Institute (2019b), 45 percent of births in the United States are unintended at the time of conception.

110
00:11:28,796 --> 00:11:34,621
But, the overall proportion of unintended births has declined since 2008.

111
00:11:34,621 --> 00:11:42,121
Unmarried women, black women, and women with less education or income are more likely to have an unplanned pregnancy.

112
00:11:42,121 --> 00:11:44,396
TABLE 1-1.

113
00:11:44,396 --> 00:11:50,758
Total Pregnancies and Outcomes in the United States in 2019 Obstetrical Care Measures.

114
00:11:50,758 --> 00:11:57,708
Several indices are used to assess obstetrical and perinatal outcomes as measures of medical care quality.

115
00:11:57,708 --> 00:12:05,671
As noted, the perinatal mortality rate includes the number of stillbirths and neonatal deaths per 1000 total births.

116
00:12:05,671 --> 00:12:10,783
In 2016, this rate was 6 deaths per 1000 births .

117
00:12:10,783 --> 00:12:14,308
This rate has been unchanged for several years .

118
00:12:14,308 --> 00:12:24,733
Rates of fetal death at 28 weeks’ gestational age or more have declined since 1990, whereas rates for those between 20 and 27 weeks are static.

119
00:12:24,733 --> 00:12:26,971
Figure 1-1.

120
00:12:26,971 --> 00:12:35,146
Perinatal, late-fetal, and early-neonatal mortality rates per 1000 births in the United States in 2016.

121
00:12:35,146 --> 00:12:44,371
Of infant deaths, the rate approximated 6 deaths per 1000 live births in 2018 compared with nearly 7 in 2001 .

122
00:12:44,371 --> 00:12:55,446
The four leading causes—congenital malformations, preterm birth, low birthweight, and maternal pregnancy complications— accounted for almost half of all infant deaths.

123
00:12:55,446 --> 00:13:02,396
Neonates born at the lowest gestational ages and birthweights add substantively to these mortality rates.

124
00:13:02,396 --> 00:13:10,258
For example, 17 percent of all infant deaths in 2018 were in those born preterm and with a low birthweight .

125
00:13:10,258 --> 00:13:18,596
Of particular interest are neonates with birthweights <500 g, for whom neonatal intensive care can now be offered .

126
00:13:18,596 --> 00:13:24,908
Of maternal deaths, rates dropped precipitously in the United States during the 20th century.

127
00:13:24,908 --> 00:13:30,846
Pregnancy-related deaths are so uncommon as to be measured per 100,000 births.

128
00:13:30,846 --> 00:13:39,308
The CDC maintains data on pregnancy-related maternal deaths in its Pregnancy Mortality Surveillance System (PMSS).

129
00:13:39,308 --> 00:13:47,471
Its latest report described 3410 pregnancy-related deaths between 2011 and 2015 .

130
00:13:47,471 --> 00:13:54,796
Approximately 5 percent were early-pregnancy maternal deaths due to ectopic gestation or abortive outcomes.

131
00:13:54,796 --> 00:14:01,958
The deadly obstetrical triad of hemorrhage, preeclampsia, and infection accounted for a third of all deaths .

132
00:14:01,958 --> 00:14:09,258
Thromboembolism, cardiomyopathy, and other cardiovascular disease together accounted for another third.

133
00:14:09,258 --> 00:14:18,908
Other significant contributors were amnionic fluid embolism (5.5 percent) and cerebrovascular accidents (8.2 percent).

134
00:14:18,908 --> 00:14:24,583
Anesthesia-related deaths were at an all time low—only 0.4 percent.

135
00:14:24,583 --> 00:14:31,408
Similar causes were reported for selected cohorts by MacDorman and associates (2017).

136
00:14:31,408 --> 00:14:33,671
Figure 1-2.

137
00:14:33,671 --> 00:14:38,446
Some causes of and their contributions to pregnancy-related maternal

138
00:14:38,596 --> 00:14:43,558
deaths in the United States from 2014–2017.

139
00:14:43,508 --> 00:14:51,521
The pregnancy-related maternal mortality ratio was 17 deaths per 100,000 live births in 2017 .

140
00:14:51,521 --> 00:14:59,971
The cause of this rise during the last 30 years may simply be that more women are dying, however, other factors explain this increase .

141
00:14:59,971 --> 00:15:07,421
First, the number of pregnant women with severe chronic health conditions, which place women at higher risk, is greater .

142
00:15:07,421 --> 00:15:14,383
Second, the increased proportion of births to women older than 40 years contributes to higher mortality rates .

143
00:15:14,383 --> 00:15:25,321
Another is an artificial elevation caused by the International Statistical Classification of Diseases, 10th Revision (ICD-10), implemented in 1999.

144
00:15:25,321 --> 00:15:34,296
Additionally, improved reporting of maternal mortality contributes to the rise (MacDorman, 2016, 2017).

145
00:15:34,296 --> 00:15:42,908
Last, implementation of the pregnancy checkbox on the death certificate was associated with an increased identification of maternal deaths .

146
00:15:42,908 --> 00:15:52,558
Thus, after accounting for the checkbox, predicted maternal mortality rates did not change significantly from 1999 through 2017.

147
00:15:52,558 --> 00:15:54,896
Figure 1-3.

148
00:15:54,896 --> 00:16:02,233
Trends in pregnancy-related maternal mortality in the United States from 1999–2017.

149
00:16:02,233 --> 00:16:11,546
Another consideration is the obvious disparity of higher mortality rates among black, Hispanic, and white women as shown in Figure 1-4.

150
00:16:11,546 --> 00:16:16,783
Racial disparities stem from health-care availability, access, or use .

151
00:16:16,783 --> 00:16:22,958
The maternal mortality rate is also disparately high in rural compared with metropolitan areas .

152
00:16:22,958 --> 00:16:25,321
Figure 1-4.

153
00:16:25,321 --> 00:16:33,521
Pregnancy-related mortality ratio by race/ethnicity in the United States from 2014–2017.

154
00:16:33,521 --> 00:16:37,883
Importantly, many maternal deaths are considered preventable.

155
00:16:37,883 --> 00:16:45,946
In one report, up to a third of pregnancy-related deaths in white women and up to half of those in black women were deemed preventable .

156
00:16:45,946 --> 00:16:53,196
One evaluation of an insured cohort reported that 28 percent of 98 maternal deaths were preventable .

157
00:16:53,196 --> 00:16:58,958
Thus, further efforts are imperative for obstetrics and described on page 6.

158
00:16:58,958 --> 00:17:01,946
# Severe Maternal Morbidity.

159
00:17:01,946 --> 00:17:09,808
This is defined as unintended events of labor and delivery resulting in serious short- or long-term consequences to a woman.

160
00:17:09,808 --> 00:17:13,483
Indicators serve as one measure to guide prevention .

161
00:17:13,483 --> 00:17:25,433
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists and the Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2016) have provided lists of suggested screening topics for this purpose.

162
00:17:25,433 --> 00:17:27,708
Table 1-2.

163
00:17:27,708 --> 00:17:39,946
Severe Maternal Morbidity Indicators To study severe maternal morbidity (SMM), the CDC analyzed more than 50 million maternity records from 1998 to 2009 .

164
00:17:39,946 --> 00:17:46,446
They reported that 129 per 10,000 women had at least one indicator for SMM .

165
00:17:46,446 --> 00:17:52,896
Thus, for every maternal death, approximately 200 women experience severe morbidity.

166
00:17:52,896 --> 00:18:04,208
As shown in Figure 1-5, SMM rates have increased during the past 15 years, and this trend is attributed to better documentation and a rise the blood transfusion rate.

167
00:18:04,208 --> 00:18:10,208
These numbers are greatest in smaller hospitals with <1000 deliveries annually .

168
00:18:10,208 --> 00:18:19,421
Last, as with mortality rates, there are serious racial and ethnic disparities for SMM, and black women are disproportionately affected .

169
00:18:19,421 --> 00:18:21,808
Figure 1-5.

170
00:18:21,808 --> 00:18:28,321
Rates of severe maternal morbidity (SMM) per 10,000 delivery hospitalizations.

171
00:18:28,321 --> 00:18:33,608
Women who received blood transfusions account for the greatest fraction of SMM.

172
00:18:33,608 --> 00:18:35,858
# Near Misses.

173
00:18:35,858 --> 00:18:41,583
Lowering medical error rates serves to diminish risks for maternal death and SMM.

174
00:18:41,583 --> 00:18:50,958
The terms near misses or close calls were introduced and defined as unplanned events caused by error that do not result in patient injury but have this potential .

175
00:18:50,958 --> 00:18:58,658
These are more common than injury events, but for obvious reasons, they are more difficult to identify and quantify.

176
00:18:58,658 --> 00:19:11,733
Systems designed to encourage reporting have been installed in various institutions and allow focused safety efforts (Clark, 2012; King, 2012; Shields, 2017).

177
00:19:11,733 --> 00:19:17,433
The World Health Organization (WHO) also implemented such a system.

178
00:19:17,433 --> 00:19:22,858
It has been validated in Brazil and accurately correlates with maternal death rates .

179
00:19:22,858 --> 00:19:31,133
A similar system in Britain is the UK Obstetric Surveillance System—UKOSS (Knight, 2005, 2008).

180
00:19:31,133 --> 00:19:40,008
In the United States, one is the National Partnership for Maternal Safety (D’Alton, 2016; Main, 2015).

181
00:19:40,180 --> 00:19:42,942
Timely Topics In Obstetrics.

182
00:19:42,892 --> 00:19:48,242
Various topics have been in the forefront since the 25th edition of this textbook.

183
00:19:48,242 --> 00:19:51,105
Here, we discuss several of these.

184
00:19:51,105 --> 00:19:54,130
# COVID-19 Pandemic.

185
00:19:54,130 --> 00:20:07,630
In early 2020, the severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS)-CoV-2 virus spread rapidly around the globe, creating the greatest public health crisis since the influenza pandemic of 1918 .

186
00:20:07,630 --> 00:20:20,130
As of early 2021, the disease caused by this virus and commonly known as COVID-19 is estimated to have infected more than 181 million people and caused nearly 4 million deaths .

187
00:20:20,130 --> 00:20:27,580
Expectedly, the healthcare and political landscapes in the United States changed dramatically because of the pandemic.

188
00:20:27,580 --> 00:20:41,755
Following the January 2020 declaration of a Global Health Emergency by the WHO, citywide lockdowns, state-mandated shelter-in-place orders, and public mask mandates were all implemented to help control early viral spread.

189
00:20:41,755 --> 00:20:48,442
Healthcare systems scrambled to acquire COVID-19 tests and personal protective equipment for staff.

190
00:20:48,442 --> 00:20:56,092
Wards dedicated solely to COVID19 care opened in hospitals throughout the nation to handle substantial patient volume.

191
00:20:56,092 --> 00:21:07,192
Despite these measures, more than 500,000 individuals—including more than 3000 healthcare workers—died in the United States in 2020 from the infection.

192
00:21:07,192 --> 00:21:13,305
Maternity wards were not spared, and traditional models of prenatal care were transformed.

193
00:21:13,305 --> 00:21:23,917
Namely, virtual care and drive-through prenatal care models aimed to reduce exposure risk to patients and staff (Holcomb, 2020; Turrentine, 2020).

194
00:21:23,917 --> 00:21:28,180
Asymptomatic or mild infections were common in pregnancy .

195
00:21:28,180 --> 00:21:37,205
Still, the effects of COVID-19 on pregnancy are not completely understood, and the effect of pregnancy on disease course is controversial.

196
00:21:37,205 --> 00:21:47,880
Management of severe COVID-19 infection in pregnancy requires interdisciplinary care and an understanding of pregnancy physiology and viral pathophysiology.

197
00:21:47,880 --> 00:21:55,805
Preventive measures—including mRNA vaccines—have been shown to be safe and highly effective in disease prevention.

198
00:21:55,805 --> 00:22:07,280
However, this critical information was delayed following exclusion of pregnant individuals from initial clinical trials (Adhikari, 2021; Polack, 2020).

199
00:22:07,280 --> 00:22:20,117
In a report describing over 800,000 pregnancies, Chinn and colleagues (2021) found that 2.2 percent (18,715) of these women had COVID-19.

200
00:22:20,117 --> 00:22:33,642
When compared with women without such infections, these women had significantly increased adverse outcomes to include preterm birth, ICU admissions, intubations and mechanical ventilation, and maternal deaths.

201
00:22:33,642 --> 00:22:39,367
In 2021 the FDA approved COVID-19 vaccines for pregnant women.

202
00:22:39,367 --> 00:22:45,680
Knowledge gained during the SARS-CoV-2 pandemic will undoubtedly shape healthcare moving forward .

203
00:22:45,680 --> 00:22:55,905
Indeed, a combined inperson plus audio-only virtual prenatal care model may most effectively provide services to vulnerable patients who lack internet access .

204
00:22:55,905 --> 00:22:59,780
# Maternal Mortality—a Call to Arms.

205
00:22:59,780 --> 00:23:08,380
Almost 700 women in the United States die each year from pregnancy or its complications, and many deaths are deemed preventable.

206
00:23:08,380 --> 00:23:14,542
As a result, obstetricians and other stakeholders have united to address these tragedies .

207
00:23:14,542 --> 00:23:24,267
Because maternal deaths are inextricably linked to SMM indicators , several programs have been designed by national organization to avoid these events.

208
00:23:24,267 --> 00:23:34,205
Noted earlier, the Pregnancy Mortality Surveillance System (PMSS) collects national pregnancy-related death data to guide prevention efforts.

209
00:23:34,205 --> 00:23:46,355
Another, the Alliance for Innovation on Maternal Health (AIM) program, creates patient safety bundles, which describe evidence-based best practices for various obstetrical settings.

210
00:23:46,355 --> 00:23:53,092
The Joint Commission recommends that birthing centers establish protocols and implement simulation efforts .

211
00:23:53,092 --> 00:24:00,992
Moreover, national working groups target specific risks, such as thromboembolism (D’Alton, 2016).

212
00:24:00,992 --> 00:24:06,042
In addition to pregnancy, the puerperium is a vulnerable period as well.

213
00:24:06,042 --> 00:24:13,480
One specific national effort is to establish dedicated 1-year postpartum follow-up to ensure ongoing care.

214
00:24:13,480 --> 00:24:22,205
Important targets are medical disorders such as hypertension, diabetes, other cardiovascular diseases, and their consequences.

215
00:24:22,205 --> 00:24:26,180
To emphasize puerperal care, the concept of a “fourth

216
00:24:26,332 --> 00:24:29,044
trimester” has been introduced .

217
00:24:28,994 --> 00:24:37,369
Moreover, legislation— the MOMMA’s Act—aims to expand Medicaid postpartum coverage from 60 days to 12 months .

218
00:24:37,369 --> 00:24:44,282
As stated by Surgeon General Jerome Adams, “We must act now; our nation and our mothers deserve better.

219
00:24:44,282 --> 00:24:47,107
# Opioid Use Disorder.

220
00:24:47,107 --> 00:25:00,632
During 1999 to 2014, the national prevalence of opioid use disorder in pregnant women rose 333 percent from 1.5 to 6.5 cases per 1000 deliveries .

221
00:25:00,632 --> 00:25:10,669
In addition to the complexities of maternal addiction, opioid use has led to an unprecedented increase in the incidence of the neonatal opioid withdrawal syndrome .

222
00:25:10,669 --> 00:25:23,144
To combat the associated adverse effects on women and their pregnancies, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019b) has stressed an active role by obstetricians.

223
00:25:23,144 --> 00:25:31,432
The College recommends universal screening by questionnaire, as well as care given to affected women by a multidisciplinary team.

224
00:25:31,432 --> 00:25:35,607
Therapeutic use of opioids is curtailed as best possible.

225
00:25:35,607 --> 00:25:44,244
Treatment of opioid use disorder with methadone or buprenorphine is challenging and discussed in Chapter 64 (p. 1150).

226
00:25:44,244 --> 00:25:50,794
Despite efforts, a significant decline in the prevalence of these disorders in gravidas is not in sight.

227
00:25:50,794 --> 00:25:54,157
# Advances in Prenatal Genetics.

228
00:25:54,157 --> 00:25:58,882
Several technologies help detect fetal genetic abnormalities.

229
00:25:58,882 --> 00:26:08,582
Since the last edition, noninvasive prenatal screening that uses cell-free DNA (cfDNA) has become commonplace in prenatal care .

230
00:26:08,582 --> 00:26:17,744
Another promising technique is chromosomal microarray analysis (CMA) performed on samples of chorionic villi or amnionic fluid.

231
00:26:17,744 --> 00:26:26,507
These tests provide sophisticated information about gains and losses of DNA segments as small as 50 to 100 kilobases.

232
00:26:26,507 --> 00:26:37,032
However, although the yield with CMA is superior to that with fetal karyotyping, most birth defects occur in the setting of normal CMA and karyotype results.

233
00:26:37,032 --> 00:26:44,757
As knowledge of the human genome has expanded, the role of specific DNA sequence abnormalities has gained attention.

234
00:26:44,757 --> 00:26:56,082
As an example, evaluation of fetal skeletal dysplasia may include panels of tests in which nextgeneration sequencing is used to identify mutations in specific genes.

235
00:26:56,082 --> 00:27:04,844
Whole exome sequencing (WES) analyzes all coding regions of DNA, which together account for 1.5 percent of the genome.

236
00:27:04,844 --> 00:27:22,094
In pregnancies with structural fetal abnormalities, and in which CMA and karyotype results are normal, WES has identified clinically significant abnormalities in approximately 10 percent of fetuses (Lord, 2019; Petrovski, 2019).

237
00:27:22,094 --> 00:27:30,807
In one series of fetuses with unexplained nonimmune hydrops, WES detected diagnostic genetic variants in nearly 30 percent .

238
00:27:30,807 --> 00:27:37,882
Although promising, WES technology at this time is not recommended for routine use in prenatal diagnosis .

239
00:27:37,882 --> 00:27:45,794
Limitations include high rates of genetic variants of uncertain significance, long turnaround times, and high costs.

240
00:27:45,794 --> 00:27:53,169
Comprehensive counseling is needed because WES may detect or suspect a finding that is unrelated but medically actionable.

241
00:27:53,169 --> 00:28:03,294
Genomic tests are reviewed in Chapter 16 (p. 324), and elements of counseling are discussed in Chapter 17 (p. 334).

242
00:28:03,294 --> 00:28:06,319
# Placenta Accreta Spectrum.

243
00:28:06,319 --> 00:28:12,969
Since our last edition, the cesarean delivery rate has been static and approximates 32 percent.

244
00:28:12,969 --> 00:28:20,469
However, rates of pregnancies complicated by placenta accreta spectrum (PAS) have grown substantially.

245
00:28:20,469 --> 00:28:25,282
An incidence as high as 1 case in 300 deliveries has been cited .

246
00:28:25,282 --> 00:28:31,782
Sequelae of these dangerous syndromes are discussed in Chapter 43 (p. 765).

247
00:28:31,782 --> 00:28:41,432
To address these risks, specialized accreta surgical teams at tertiary care centers and greater antepartum transfer to these centers are both on the rise.

248
00:28:41,432 --> 00:28:47,294
As one prevention, national efforts have worked to avoid the primary cesarean delivery.

249
00:28:47,294 --> 00:28:54,007
However, despite these efforts, PAS will likely continue as a significant risk for SMM.

250
00:28:54,007 --> 00:28:57,832
# Progestogens to Prevent Preterm Birth.

251
00:28:57,832 --> 00:29:03,144
Progesterone derivatives to forestall preterm birth have been studied for decades.

252
00:29:03,144 --> 00:29:19,544
One—intramuscular 17-alpha-hydroxyprogesterone caproate (17OHPC)—was approved by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) under the accelerated approval process and contingent on demonstration of efficacy in a second

253
00:29:19,708 --> 00:29:21,120
trial.

254
00:29:21,070 --> 00:29:32,370
The drug is marketed as Makena, and subsequent, observational studies, described in Chapter 45 (p. 795), have led to questions of its efficacy .

255
00:29:32,370 --> 00:29:44,533
In 2019, results of the confirmatory trial of Makena efficacy—the PROLONG trial—failed to show its benefits compared with placebo for prevention of birth before 35 weeks .

256
00:29:44,533 --> 00:29:52,458
Later in 2019, an FDA Advisory Committee voted 9 to 7 to withdraw interim accelerated approval.

257
00:29:52,458 --> 00:30:04,758
Analyses by the committee included cross-study comparisons and subgroup analyses that did not show 17-OHPC benefits (Fig. 45-6, p. 796).

258
00:30:04,758 --> 00:30:13,445
In late 2020, the FDA Center for Drug Evaluation and Research (CDER) recommended drug withdrawal from the market.

259
00:30:13,445 --> 00:30:25,358
Subsequently, obstetricians became polarized regarding “off label” use of the drug because it appears safe (Chang, 2020; Greene, 2020; Sibai, 2020).

260
00:30:25,358 --> 00:30:41,695
Despite findings from the PROLONG trial and the FDA’s CDER, both the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2021) and the Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2020) continued to endorse 17-OHPC use.

261
00:30:41,695 --> 00:30:49,220
This, however, is with the proviso that “uncertainty regarding benefit” be shared with the patient during decision-making.

262
00:30:49,220 --> 00:30:58,945
Last, the EPPPIC Group (2021) performed a metaanalysis of randomized trials evaluating progestogens for preterm birth prevention.

263
00:30:58,945 --> 00:31:08,545
Although not statistically significant, they concluded progestogens, which include 17-OHPC, reduced births at less than 34 weeks.

264
00:31:08,545 --> 00:31:16,620
The FDA’s CDER (2021) continues to recommend withdrawal of 17-OHPC from the market.

265
00:31:16,620 --> 00:31:24,658
At this time, however, thousands of women continue to receive 17-OHPC despite its questionable efficacy.

